[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The best way to save costs on your AI agents is not what you think. Just dropping down the model and hoping for the same results is actually not that reliable or effective and it could actually lead to extended run time if that weaker model doesn\'t have the depth of intelligence required to complete the'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'task. Instead, to really optimize cost without compromising quality, you need to focus on codifying the agent\'s job into skills, memories and scripts. Let me show you exactly what I mean. So most agent cost waste comes from asking our'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'agents to rediscover the same things over and over. Modern LLMs are so eager to please, they\'re willing and able to figure out the same things from scratch every run, but this is where a lot of token waste and cost overrun actually'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comes from. Okay, so let\'s take a look in HyperAgent here. I have different versions of the same agent and I ran it a few different times with different configurations to try to see which one holds quality consistent while finding some meaningful cost savings. So this'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'agent is called Penny. It is a content strategist for a brand called BrightLeaf. BrightLeaf is all about making personal finance more human. Penny\'s job is to go and search the web for trending topics within the personal'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'finance niche. It\'s going to look at Reddit, X, Twitter, Substack. It\'s going to find news with a finance hook and then it\'s going to pick a topic that\'s aligned with our brand\'s voice, what we stand for, what we typically cover and'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'develop a take related to kind of what we stand for as a brand. Then Penny is going to deliver that content piece in the form of a long-form article like this one that has some kind of interactive tool within it that will'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'allow our audience to bring this concept to life. So maybe there\'s like some kind of slider or calculator or something like that. So this is the first time I ran Penny. I just said run content strategy for the week and Penny looked'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through the system prompt based on the job that I gave it. Penny wrote that system prompt for itself, by the way. That was about a dollar ninety to just get Penny created. So pretty trivial there, but actually running the job, performing the web research, you can see'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here Penny actually created different sub agents that went off this one called Sprig went and looked at Reddit, Twig went and looked at X and Substack, and then Bramble went and looked at news.'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Those sub agents were all using Opus. Penny itself is using Opus here. It chose a topic. In this case, it\'s saying you know, let\'s do a deep dive on what\'s actually in your index fund and how concentrated it\'s become and the risk'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that that presents. So, Penny came up with a take on that, created the tool, created the long-form piece, and then this is the result we got. I had a couple small visual tweaks for Penny,'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but it was pretty much one shot, maybe a 1.1 shot if you like. So, the total cost for this thread using Opus as the main agent, using Opus for the sub agent was $11.53.'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But, the key thing to note here was that at this point Penny did not have any skills. Skills are of course this way to codify a repeatable step or a technique that you want this agent to be able to'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do and go back to again and again, and perform it largely the same way each time. That is where you\'re going to find your cost savings. So, this first run of Penny with no skills, it had to figure out a lot of things'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from scratch. It had to figure out exactly how to get access to the right information on Reddit, X, and news, how to come up with kind of like a visual design system for the data visualization in the tool. But, once it has done this job once, now Penny can create skills to'

[03:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'codify that learning. So, take 100 tokens of reasoning and collapse it down to something like 10 tokens of reusing that same skill. And I didn\'t have to go and reason about what exactly should be'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'repeatable in Penny\'s process, I just asked Penny. I said, "What are five things you did in this thread that could become repeatable skills or scripts to help you reduce the need to figure those'

[03:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'things out next time?" Penny self-reflected and came back with an analysis and said this Trend Scout harness I had to handwrite three parallel sub agent prompts that could be codified into a skill called the weekly'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scout. The article HTML template I built the entire scaffold for that. I shouldn\'t have to do that from scratch every time. I should have a template I can write to again and again. That becomes a skill. The brand voice market data freshness fetcher anything that the agent had to'

[04:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'think deeply about for this run it\'s going to be able to reflect and say that should be a skill. So now here\'s our cost optimized version of Penny and this is where things get really interesting'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because even by keeping Penny on Opus 47 but giving Penny skills to rerun those repeatable aspects of the job now we\'re actually going to find some real cost'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'savings. So now here is a new and improved version of Penny that is more optimized for cost because of what we\'ve codified into skills. So look at the five skills right here. Let\'s take this one the BrightLeaf weekly scout. We can'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'click into that skill. We can understand exactly what Penny has codified for itself right here. There\'s information on when to use this skill. So run this at the start of every content cycle. The actual documentation of the skill right'

[05:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here. There\'s a specific script and schema that that Penny has learned to standardize around. The full script is right here in the documentation and actually as a property as part of the'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'metadata of the skill is the script itself. So skills are still kind of describing tasks in plain language the way that our system prompts or the way that we work with any agent tends to do'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but with scripts now we\'re actually dealing in code. So scripts are completely deterministic. So rather than have to invent a script every time we have a script as part of a skill. A skill describes the job in natural'

[05:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'language and a script is a tool written in code that this agent can use to run that exact same task the same way each time. If it needs to tweak the script based on some specific situation it\'s'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in, it can do that within the context of the skill, but the skills and scripts are actually where our cost leverage is going to come from. So, let\'s actually appreciate this cost difference now. So, this is a second run that I did of Penny. It chose a new topic. It had to'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do the research from scratch, and it created a totally new long-form piece for us right here in our brand style, and another interactive tool. This one is a a federal student loan plan'

[06:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comparison tool or calculator, it looks like. The cost of this one, less than half of the first run, 498. And that\'s staying with Opus 4 7, staying with the most powerful frontier model at the time'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of recording this, but codifying the core repeatable aspects of this job into skills, that is where the cost leverage comes from. But now, if you\'re wondering, well, what does it look like if we do run Sonnet for our main agent? It\'s actually a pretty trivial'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'difference in terms of cost. It\'s just 428. So, that tells us that the real leverage is not coming from just dropping down the model, because in fact, if there is some ambiguity still remaining in the task or based on this'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'particular run of this job, there\'s more reasoning to do, a model that locks the depth of intelligence is going to need to look run longer, is probably going to hit more errors than the most powerful'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'frontier model running as our main agent. So, we ran the same agent with three different setups. Again, the first one was with no skills, contexts, or scripts, and we used Opus 4 7 to run this agent, 1153. And then after that'

[07:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first run, we asked that agent to self-reflect on the things that should become skills and scripts for more economical reuse in the future. We ran that cost-optimized agent still on Opus'

[07:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '4 7 for 498, and got a great outcome. And then just to complete the picture, we took that cost-optimized agent that had all the skills, dropped it down to Sonnet. What\'s the cost implication of'

[07:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that? Well, it\'s pretty trivial, it\'s pretty marginal at that point, just down to 428. And if we want to get picky about the output of the Sonnet one, there are some things in the UI and UX here that are a little bit janky. These'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'boxes are kind of overlapping. Um this could be probably a better use of space. And so, we\'re we\'re taking a little bit of a compromise on the quality of the output by dropping the main agent down to Sonnet at that point for very, very'

[08:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'marginal cost savings. So, that is why the best way to find cost savings with your AI agent is not just by dropping the model down, it is actually by codifying the reusable steps and techniques that that agent needs into'

[08:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'skills, scripts, and memories. Hope that helps.'
